WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:08.614
A payment request allocates parsed JSON, a few data objects, some strings and a response.

2
00:00:08.614 --> 00:00:11.737
A few milliseconds later, all of it is garbage.

3
00:00:11.933 --> 00:00:17.423
A few objects live much longer, like a cached merchant config or a pooled connection.

4
00:00:18.233 --> 00:00:22.832
That split has a name, the weak generational hypothesis.

5
00:00:22.832 --> 00:00:24.720
Most objects die young.

6
00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:31.864
In one real collection, the young objects were 27 MB still alive and 470 MB of garbage.

7
00:00:32.667 --> 00:00:35.559
HotSpot's collectors split the heap to match.

8
00:00:35.559 --> 00:00:38.773
New objects go into eden, in the young generation.

9
00:00:38.773 --> 00:00:42.180
Objects that stay alive end up in the old generation.

10
00:00:42.367 --> 00:00:46.875
Your code fills eden with new objects, and most of them die soon after.

11
00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:49.719
When eden fills, a young collection starts.

12
00:00:49.719 --> 00:00:54.836
It follows references from the roots and finds the few objects that are still alive.

13
00:00:55.033 --> 00:01:00.068
It copies them into an empty survivor space, and each one's age goes up by one.

14
00:01:00.068 --> 00:01:02.171
Then all of eden is free at once.

15
00:01:02.171 --> 00:01:05.421
The collector never even looks at the dead objects.

16
00:01:05.633 --> 00:01:11.315
So a young collection costs time in proportion to what survives, not to what was allocated.

17
00:01:12.333 --> 00:01:13.442
There is a catch.

18
00:01:13.442 --> 00:01:17.226
Moving an object is only safe while no thread is using it.

19
00:01:17.226 --> 00:01:25.250
So first, every application thread stops at a safepoint, a known place where the collector can find and fix its references.

20
00:01:25.250 --> 00:01:27.272
That is a stop-the-world pause.

21
00:01:27.467 --> 00:01:31.087
In G1, every young collection runs inside such a pause.

22
00:01:31.087 --> 00:01:37.933
This time the live objects from eden and from the survivor space are copied to the other survivor space.

23
00:01:37.933 --> 00:01:39.711
Then the threads run again.

24
00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:43.922
The age lives in four bits of each object's header.

25
00:01:43.922 --> 00:01:51.933
After enough survivals, at most 15, an object is promoted to the old generation, which is collected much less often.

26
00:01:52.133 --> 00:01:55.667
This is why medium-lived objects are the expensive kind.

27
00:01:55.667 --> 00:02:04.755
They get copied at every young collection, then promoted, and then they die in the old generation, where only a bigger collection can free them.

28
00:02:04.967 --> 00:02:09.773
An idempotency cache with a 10-minute expiry has exactly that shape.
